# rov_control_system

***###smoothing.py***_

It contains two kinds of smoothing (linear / exponential).
They calculate values as floats and you can change their speed by changing (step_size / alpha)

The step() function : tracks joystick movement by comparing its previous and current values, so it can determine how the ROV speed should change

The switch_strategy() function : allows you to choose the smoothing strategy you want to use

***###smoothing_interface.py***_

Allows this class to be treated like a function, making it easy to call

###pmw_mapper.py
turn the [-1 , 1]

into real numbers the motor can take as speed

###joystick.py
I used the same code from the OOP task to detect which key is pressed.

this class It includes a cleanup function to clear the terminal so there aren’t any leftover values
 get_place function checks where the joystick was last placed 

 get_button calls the function that identifies which key is being pressed  (_*poll_*keyboard)

###navigation.py
It has functions that call all the functions made in other classes and it prints the returned values.

All the actual logic: map -> smooth -> apply

###joystick.node.py

this class subscribe to topic joystick take the published values from the joystick_node

and after it  applay the  logic: map -> smooth -> apply

it publish this values to topic called thruster_pwm

and print the values

###navigation_node.py

It contains a node called joystick_node that publishes the joystick position to a topic called joystick by reading the values of the pressed keys.

if you what to change the smoothing strategy

```python
        # Navigation Service with smoothing strategy
        self.service = NavigationServiceImpl(
            self.thruster_ids, 
            smoothing_strategy="exponential", 
            alpha=0.2
        )

```

Set (smoothing_strategy) to the strategy you want.

If you want linear smoothing 

change the smoothing_strategy to = “linear”

and change (alpha) to (step_size).

example:
 alpha: float = 0.2  for exponential

step_size: float = 20.0  for  linear

This project includes only 2 strategies: "linear" and "exponential"

